WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:15.349
SHOW ONLY THE LA LAYER
In the Explorer, ⊞ on LA scopes every diagram to the logical-architecture package. The chip above the canvas says what is shown.

2
00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:28.487
GENERAL VIEW
All of LA, left to right: 21 logical functions, each realising an SA function and allocated to a component, the state machines and the hazards. Nothing physical yet.

3
00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:41.536
READ ONE ELEMENT
Select a box: Properties shows what it is. fleet is the twelve identical drones, typed by SwarmMember. It gets 14 functions, the 8 coordination ones among them.

4
00:00:41.536 --> 00:00:54.688
ADD A PART BACK
Before recording we deleted memberB, the second representative drone. Now we add it back. A new part goes under the selection, so first select LA in the Explorer.

5
00:00:54.688 --> 00:01:03.102
PLACE A PART
Pick Part in the palette, then click empty canvas. The new part is created in LA and selected.

6
00:01:03.102 --> 00:01:11.757
NAME IT
Type its name in Properties. With no type yet, memberB sits apart, among the boxes nothing connects to.

7
00:01:11.757 --> 00:01:24.489
TYPE IT
In Properties, Type: SwarmMember, then Enter. The typing is bound, as the text's memberB : SwarmMember: its typed-by line is drawn and memberB moves beside memberA.

8
00:01:24.489 --> 00:01:36.714
DOCUMENT IT
Its documentation goes in Properties, word for word as in the model: the second representative member, the other end of the peer link.

9
00:01:36.715 --> 00:01:50.570
WIRE THE PEER LINK
In Interconnection, memberA and memberB show the ports a SwarmMember has. Pick Interface, click memberA's meshOut, then memberB's meshIn: the interface ends on the ports.

10
00:01:50.570 --> 00:02:05.891
NAME IT AND TYPE IT
In Properties: the name peerLink and the type Common::MeshInterface, then Enter. The Text tab now has interface peerLink : …MeshInterface connect memberA.meshOut to memberB.meshIn, as before.

11
00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:17.217
THE RESULT
memberB is back beside memberA, typed by SwarmMember and joined to it by peerLink. Not re-entered: PA's trace to memberB, which lives in PA.

12
00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:28.499
TREE VIEW
What LA contains, one branch at a time. +N on SwarmMember opens what each member carries: its nine ports and its two state machines.

13
00:02:28.499 --> 00:02:45.431
INTERCONNECTION VIEW
Components with ports on their edges. The eleven lines are connections: peerLink between the two representatives; fleet sends reports to triageUnit, status and tracks to groundStation; tasking and recall come back.

14
00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:59.290
ACTION VIEW
The 21 logical functions, each allocated to a component: a set, not a sequence. Unlike SA, LA models no flows between them; what passes between components is on the connections.

15
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:12.036
STATE VIEW
Five state machines: a member's sortie and its condition, the ground station's duty and its link, and the mesh across the fleet. No transition has a trigger yet.

16
00:03:12.036 --> 00:03:23.564
REQUIREMENT VIEW
The eight hazards this layer adds, all satisfied by fleet and four also by a function. The constraint at the top right is drawn here too.

17
00:03:23.564 --> 00:03:39.562
PARAMETRIC VIEW
One box: LA's coverage arithmetic, its equation in the box. It derives the coverage estimate from LA's own numbers, 12 × (40 − 7.41) / 60 × 0.12 = 0.78, short of the 0.9 target; the estimate's doc says so.
